Zach from the try guys has come out and  said that he has an autoimmune condition  I'm about to watch the video and react  to it let's get to it I have had really  bad back shoes basically  our life perfect I'm an osteopathic  physician this is gonna go really well  my theory is that a lot of the scoliosis  that kids are developing these days come  from uneven backpacks wearing them on  one shoulder or even those desks where  you can only lean on one side I ache I I  get stiff it kind of started before I  went to BuzzFeed a lot of my time would  be spent at a computer and I noticed I  would just when I sneezed I could feel  the pain shoot from my back through the  front through my chest cage the way that  he's describing his pain right away  tells me that there's some kind of  inflammation happening at the rib joints  because remember the ribs attached to  the front of your chest the sternum at  least the upper ribs here and then also  to your spine in the back the thoracic  spine I have a bit of a hypochondria and  so whenever something's wrong with me  I'm like cancer it's cancer cancer so I  told my doctor about this pain he says  that's not a thing you're good if you're  a doctor and a patient's coming in with  a high level of anxiety about a  condition and you know they're  susceptible to health related anxiety  you have to right away be ultra  transparent and honest about what you're  doing and why you're doing it not just  give statements like you're good and  move on I decided it was time to go to  the chiropractor we took an x-ray turns  out I feel scoliosis so scoliosis  doesn't always require treatment we  blame a lot of problems on patients  scoliosis when it's actually not the  main cause it's very easy in a patient  who's having back pain caused by  something completely different to get an  x-ray and blame it all on the scoliosis  in fact it's the lazy thing to do a  small degree of scoliosis shouldn't be  causing significant discomfort in fact  most cases of scoliosis that we see are  extremely mild and are asymptomatic now  for anyone who has scoliosis they will  be the first people to tell you that my  scoliosis is minor it's not because you  would get surgery for I tried a lot of  things to help this I changed my diet I  don't know what the diet would help with  scoliosis with unless he had a bone  mineral deficiency there the main thing  that you do for mild scoliosis like this  is not even bracing not surgery  obviously Physical Therapy doing some  exercise some lifestyle modifications  make sure you're not wearing a book bag  on only on one side the pain built to a  point where I couldn't sleep I woke up  at like 4 45 in pain this was every  night for for months  laying down hurt uh being in my bed  hurt I mean living  hurt I'm talking about I would wake up  in the middle of the night five times  and all during this I am I'm working a  full-time job but when you're not  sleeping it it eats away at you mentally  it absolutely does because you don't  recover well you're constantly in a  hyper sympathetic state where it's like  you're basically being chased by a lion  you're never relaxing you're never  digesting you're never repairing and  healing and as a doctor you have to ask  these things because when we ask how  much disability a certain injury is  causing you it's not about whether or  not you can walk or run or lift or not  it's also about can you rest adequately  can you sleep well can you sit well can  you perform the duties at your job well  I start screaming I think and I feel bad  for the doctor but I was like hey this  can't continue I'm losing my mind I got  referred to a rheumatologist and within  minutes the rheumatologist is like oh  yeah I think I know what's wrong with  you and I'm like what I'm interested  what conversation they had in the  medical office that led him to go to see  a rheumatologist who's essentially an  autoimmune joint disease expert why they  went that route versus a spinal surgeon  for example it could have been that he  wasn't interested in spine surgery but  there has to have been tip-offs to some  sort of autoimmune process I had to go  in for an MRI which was scary because at  that point I didn't know what was wrong  with me he just referred me to an MRI  and I'm like oh God what is this MRI is  magnetic resonance imaging where you  have giant magnets essentially spin  around you to give us really good  quality images it's really loud it's  very claustrophobic some of my patients  actually need benzodiazepine medications  which are anti-anxiety medications to  even be able to get an MRI they do a  great job at showing soft tissue like an  x-ray for example will show bone quite  well or joints well the MRI would show  us cartilage tendons ligaments organs it  turns out I have Ankylosing Spondylitis  Ankylosing Spondylitis is basically an  inflammatory autoimmune condition where  it usually starts in the sacroiliac  joint which is the joint between your  hips and one of the lower portions of  your spine your sacrum it can get so bad  that it starts spreading to other  vertebral joints moving up your back  where you develop stiffness and in the  worst conditions actually the joints  fuse and you become very stiff it  becomes very painful think about  inflammation in the joint this was about  four months ago I am happy to report  that since that time I've been on  medication and I have felt better than I  have felt since like childhood I take a  drug called Humira so no matter where I  am no matter what I'm doing I have to  stop what I'm doing and give myself a  little shot  you ready to get poked we hold on to  Zach's medicine I have to take this  medication twice a month at the same  time so uh it's 8 40 a.m and I gotta  take this shot this is a medication  that's used as a disease modifying  anti-rheumatic drug and these dmards  work through different processes but the  medication he takes targets tumor  necrosis factor and it actually allows  the decrease of inflammation where he  has an unnecessary increase and so  what's great is that you never actually  see the needle oh right that's nice see  this window right here some yellow  liquid is going to pop out of it  but that's it now I'm done so I started  my medication and for a few months  my life was incredible I was able to  sleep throughout the night without  waking up in pain I wasn't stiff  throughout the day it's important to  remember as he's talking about his  experience with this medication that is  not a cure for his condition it's a  disease modifying drug so it changes the  course the development making the  progression slower that's the most  important takeaway during the time I was  instructed to keep up with physical  activity but as the months pass  I  got a little LAX I was back to where I  was the truth is I started ignoring my  disease the second I could the moment  that my medicine started working I  paying attention to it so it was time to  go back to the doctor finally ready to  accept and confront my symptoms and  ready to attack this sickness head on  and  he said I was fine  my inflammatory markers were still low  if a patient is coming in and saying  they're suffering again with their  illness that they feel like it's making  a return they're going back to where  they were and I come in and I say no  your inflammatory cells are good  that's not adequate medical care we  never treat lab values we don't treat  inflammatory cells if a patient's coming  in and telling me they have back pain if  a patient's telling me that they have  terrible crippling anxiety and  depressive symptoms I don't say oh your  Labs look good so Deuces I decided to  make my health a full-time job  Physical Therapy twice a week yoga and  stretching first thing when I wake up  and last thing before I go to bed long  box with Bowie every single day instead  of letting someone else walk him for me  and exercise especially on the days that  I don't want to accepting that sometimes  being late to work in the morning is a  necessary component to my health the  movement component and the healing that  comes from moving is amazing because  whether it's a medication a massage  physical therapy all of that is in place  so your body heals itself and a body  that's not in motion will continue  staying not in motion and continue being  in pain and after all that  I feel okay  I still have a baseline of pain most  days I don't know that I'm ever gonna  fully be a hundred percent again  but I am done ignoring my pain that's  really powerful because acceptance is a  big part of chronic pain a lot of times  we promise patients that we can fully  get rid of their pain go from a hundred  to zero and a lot of times that's not  feasible most notably so when it comes  to opioid treatments patients that are  promised zero pain are giving opioids  their pain goes to zero but then we  can't continue those in many cases long  term unless that there's an end point  there's a goal for something check out  my video of Michael Reeves and his  bladder exploding what click it it's  It's the Most Fascinating story he's  actually in the video with me click here  to check that out and as always stay  happy and healthy
 

